<h1>Video 096 — java.util.concurrent package (locks intro)</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: Multithreading Enhancement  Part- 2|| java.util.concurrent package</p>
<p>ASR decode (this lecture): keep subing = keep writing (board heading); synchronizer / synchronizer keyword = synchronized; luck / L do = lock / l.; trock / tryck / TR = tryLock; religious lock = releases lock; thre / threats = thread(s); loocks / logs package = locks package; reenter / reentrant = ReentrantLock; interrupta = lockInterruptibly; illegal monitor State = IllegalMonitorStateException; Nan / microc = NANOSECONDS / MICROSECONDS; legend = listen; keep uping = keep writing.</p>
<h3>00:07 — Topic: `java.util.concurrent` / locks package</h3>
<p>Board heading: java.util.concurrent package (and its sub-package locks). Last sessions covered the synchronized keyword. There are many problems with traditional synchronization.</p>
<h3>00:38 — Biggest problems with synchronized</h3>
<ol><li>Performance goes down.</li></ol>
<ol><li>If synchronized is not used properly → chance of deadlock. Synchronized is the only reason deadlock situations appear in the classic model.</li></ol>
<p>To overcome these: `java.util.concurrent.locks` came into the picture (sub-package of java.util.concurrent). He will explain locks in detail.</p>
<h3>01:17 — Problem 1: no try-for-lock without waiting</h3>
<p>Requirement: “I want to try for the lock. If available, give it. If not available, I won’t wait — I will do alternative operations.”</p>
<p>Traditional synchronized: until you get the lock you must wait. No alternative. Waiting time unknown (1 minute, 10 minutes, 1 hour, 10 days).</p>
<p>Bus analogy: synchronized = wait for the bus forever. Flexible approach = ask “bus available?” — if yes, board; if no, take another path.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Conceptual flexibility NOT available with synchronized alone:
Lock l = /* some Lock */;
if (l.tryLock()) {
    // got lock → safe operations
} else {
    // lock not available → alternative operations (no wait)
}</pre>
<h3>04:02 — No waiting ⇒ no deadlock question</h3>
<p>If you do not wait when the lock is unavailable, there is no waiting time → no question of deadlock. In the concurrent locks package this style of API exists.</p>
<h3>04:27 — Problem 2: no fairness / no control who gets the lock</h3>
<p>If T1, T2, T3 wait for a lock and the lock becomes free — which thread gets it? Unknown (thread scheduler). You may want: longest waiting thread must get it (fairness policy). Synchronized cannot guarantee this. In locks there is a fairness flag; if set, longest waiting thread gets the chance.</p>
<p>“Be a bit fair / don’t do fraud” = fairness policy.</p>
<h3>05:41 — Problem 3: no API for waiting-thread count</h3>
<p>How many threads are waiting for the lock? Synchronized has no API to list/count waiting threads.</p>
<h3>06:03 — Board: problems with traditional synchronized keyword</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Board heading:
// Problems with traditional synchronized keyword</pre>
<h3>07:00 — Problem list dictated on board (1)</h3>
<p>1. No flexibility to try for a lock without waiting.</p>
<h3>07:38 — Problem list (2): no max waiting time</h3>
<p>Sometimes you want: wait at most 10 minutes; if still no lock, continue with alternative work. Synchronized: wait until lock forever — no max waiting time → performance problems and deadlock risk.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// 2. There is no way to specify maximum waiting time
//    for your thread to get lock
//    → thread will wait until getting the lock
//    → which may create performance problems
//    → which may cause deadlock</pre>
<h3>09:44 — Problem list (3): who gets the lock after release</h3>
<p>If a thread releases the lock, which waiting thread gets it? No control with synchronized.</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// 3. If a thread releases lock, then which waiting thread
//    will get that lock — we are not having any control on this</pre>
<h3>10:34 — Problem list (4): no API to list waiting threads</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// 4. There is no API to list out all waiting threads for a lock</pre>
<h3>11:08 — Problem list (5): synchronized only method-level or block-inside-method</h3>
<p>Ways to use synchronized:</p>
<ul><li>declare a method synchronized</li></ul>
<ul><li>or a synchronized block inside a method (synchronized(this) etc.)</li></ul>
<p>It applies at method level or within the method. Across multiple methods you cannot “start lock in M1 and release inside M2” cleanly with the keyword alone.</p>
<p>Scenario: need lock from a point in M1 through part of called M2, then release mid-flow so later code is open to other threads. With Lock API:</p>
<pre class='code'>Lock l = /* ... */;

void m1() {
    l.lock();
    // need lock here
    m2();
}

void m2() {
    // still under lock from m1&#x27;s acquisition...
    // from some point lock no longer required:
    l.unlock();
    // multiple threads allowed from here
}</pre>
<p>Across multiple methods, synchronization nature is achievable with `java.util.concurrent.locks`, not with traditional synchronized keyword alone.</p>
<h3>13:32 — Board dictation of problem 5</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// 5. Compulsory we have to use synchronized either at
//    method level or within the method
//    and it is not possible to use across multiple methods</pre>
<h3>14:28 — Five problems → `java.util.concurrent.locks` in 1.5</h3>
<p>Total five problems listed. To overcome them, Sun introduced `java.util.concurrent.locks` package in 1.5.</p>
<p>Package also provides several enhancements for more control on concurrency (not only “alternative to synchronized”).</p>
<h3>16:42 — Need of Lock: interface + implementation class</h3>
<p>In this package topic he focuses on:</p>
<ul><li>interface: `Lock`</li></ul>
<ul><li>implementation class: `ReentrantLock`</li></ul>
<p>Methods already mentioned verbally: tryLock, lock, unlock — live on Lock / ReentrantLock.</p>
<h3>17:32 — Lock object vs implicit lock</h3>
<p>A Lock (implementation) object is similar to the implicit lock a thread acquires for a synchronized method/block — but created explicitly, and Lock implementations provide more extensive operations than traditional implicit locks (e.g. how many waiting threads, unlock when done, etc.).</p>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// Lock object is similar to implicit lock acquired by a thread
// to execute synchronized method or synchronized block
//
// Lock implementations provide more extensive operations
// than traditional implicit locks</pre>
<h3>19:56 — Important methods of Lock interface — `void lock()`</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;

// l.lock();
void lock();</pre>
<p>Use to acquire a lock:</p>
<ul><li>If lock available → current thread gets it immediately and continues.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If lock not available → wait until getting the lock.</li></ul>
<p>Exactly same behavior as traditional synchronized keyword (wait forever).</p>
<h3>23:08 — Motivation story then `boolean tryLock()`</h3>
<p>Long classroom story (ideas vs try vs 1% who continue; sea waves near shore vs calm in middle; Indians / software companies) → moral: just try once.</p>
<pre class='code'>boolean tryLock();</pre>
<ul><li>If available → acquire and return `true`.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If not available → return `false`; thread continues without waiting.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Thread never enters waiting state for this call.</li></ul>
<p>Usage pattern he writes:</p>
<pre class='code'>Lock l = /* ... */;

if (l.tryLock()) {
    // perform safe operations
} else {
    // perform alternative operations
}</pre>
<h3>28:51 — Board: meaning of tryLock()</h3>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// boolean tryLock()
// to acquire the lock without waiting
// if the lock is available → thread acquires that lock and returns true
// if the lock is not available → this method returns false
//   and can continue its execution without waiting
// in this case thread never be entered into waiting state</pre>
<h3>31:23 — Compare lock vs tryLock; introduce timed tryLock</h3>
<ul><li>l.lock() → available? get; not? wait until get.</li></ul>
<ul><li>l.tryLock() → available? get; not? don’t wait, continue.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Medium form: try; if not available wait some time (e.g. 1 hour); still not? continue.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;

// wait up to 1 hour then give up
boolean ok = l.tryLock(1, TimeUnit.HOURS);</pre>
<h3>32:55 — Third method: `boolean tryLock(long time, TimeUnit unit)`</h3>
<pre class='code'>boolean tryLock(long time, TimeUnit unit) throws InterruptedException;</pre>
<p>Board meaning:</p>
<ul><li>If lock available → get lock and continue.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If not available → wait until specified amount of time.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Still not available after that → thread can continue execution (returns false).</li></ul>
<h3>35:25 — `TimeUnit` enum</h3>
<p>TimeUnit is an enum (group of constants) in `java.util.concurrent` (not in the locks sub-package).</p>
<p>He runs javap on java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit — shown as abstract class at API level but strictly an enum. Allowed values:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

// Allowed TimeUnit values (board):
// NANOSECONDS
// MICROSECONDS
// MILLISECONDS
// SECONDS
// MINUTES
// HOURS
// DAYS</pre>
<p>Lock interface is in java.util.concurrent.locks; TimeUnit is in java.util.concurrent — import both when needed.</p>
<h3>39:03 — Example call with milliseconds</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;

if (l.tryLock(1000, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS)) {
    // got the lock within 1000 ms
} else {
    // still no lock after waiting up to 1000 ms
}</pre>
<h3>39:51 — Fourth method: `void lockInterruptibly()`</h3>
<p>Spelling care: lockInterruptibly.</p>
<pre class='code'>void lockInterruptibly() throws InterruptedException;</pre>
<ul><li>If lock available → acquire and return immediately.</li></ul>
<ul><li>If not available → wait.</li></ul>
<ul><li>While waiting, if the thread is interrupted → thread won’t get the lock (interrupted while waiting).</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>// Board:
// void lockInterruptibly()
// acquires the lock if it is available and returns immediately
// if the lock is not available then it will wait
// while waiting if the thread is interrupted
//   then thread won&#x27;t get the lock</pre>
<h3>42:12 — Fifth method: `void unlock()`</h3>
<pre class='code'>void unlock();</pre>
<p>Releases the lock.</p>
<p>Twist: only the owner of the lock may call unlock.</p>
<p>Lock l = /* ... */;

// OK: owner unlocks
l.lock();
// safe operations
l.unlock();

// BAD: main calls unlock without owning the lock
l.unlock();
// Runtime: IllegalMonitorStateException</p>
<p>Board rule:</p>
<pre class='code'>// To call unlock(), compulsory current thread should be
// owner of the lock
// otherwise we will get runtime exception saying
// IllegalMonitorStateException</pre>
<h3>45:03 — Five methods of Lock; next class ReentrantLock</h3>
<p>Methods of Lock interface (five):</p>
<ol><li>lock()</li></ol>
<ol><li>tryLock() (no time)</li></ol>
<ol><li>tryLock(long, TimeUnit) (with time)</li></ol>
<ol><li>lockInterruptibly()</li></ol>
<ol><li>unlock()</li></ol>
<p>Implementation class for next session: `ReentrantLock`.</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.util.concurrent.locks.Lock;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

Lock l = new ReentrantLock();</pre>
